<section class="section">
  <div class="container" style="max-width:980px">

    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Where WordPress can still make sense</h2>
    <p>WordPress is the only platform on this comparison list with use cases I will straightforwardly recommend it for:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>You publish constantly and the writing matters more than the chrome.</strong> WordPress's editor (Gutenberg) is competent for high-volume publishing. If you are writing 5+ posts a week and the site exists primarily to serve that writing, a CMS with a real editor is the right shape.</li>
      <li><strong>You have a high-volume editorial team.</strong> If 10 people each need to log in and write, WordPress's user roles are mature. Custom static sites cannot match that workflow without bolting on a headless CMS (which is fine, but it is engineering).</li>
      <li><strong>You need a specific plugin that exists only on WordPress.</strong> Membership sites, complex e-commerce with WooCommerce, learning management. The plugin ecosystem is a real strength.</li>
      <li><strong>You already have a WordPress site running well, with a maintained host.</strong> Migrating off WordPress to a custom site for the sake of it is usually not worth it. Migrating because the site is slow, hacked, or unmaintainable is a different story.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where WordPress hurts you</h2>
    <p>For a small service business that needs a website to bring in calls, WordPress is structurally the wrong tool:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Security is constant work.</strong> Every plugin is a potential vulnerability. WordPress sites are the #1 target on the internet by a wide margin. Without active maintenance (plugin updates, core updates, security plugin, malware monitoring), getting hacked is a question of when, not if.</li>
      <li><strong>Page speed is uphill.</strong> WordPress page-builders (Elementor, Divi, etc.) emit hundreds of KB of unused CSS and JavaScript. Real-world WordPress sites typically score 30&ndash;65 on Google PageSpeed mobile. Hitting 90+ requires substantial caching, optimization, and developer work.</li>
      <li><strong>Plugin sprawl is a tax.</strong> A typical service-business WordPress site runs 15&ndash;30 plugins. Each plugin has updates, maintenance, conflicts, and CVEs. The hosting bill is one number; the ongoing maintenance hours are another, and they do not appear until something breaks.</li>
      <li><strong>You depend on a chain of vendors.</strong> Hosting (Bluehost, GoDaddy, Kinsta), theme (StudioPress, Astra, custom), page builder (Elementor, Beaver), backup (UpdraftPlus), security (Wordfence), SEO (Yoast or Rank Math), forms (Gravity Forms, Contact Form 7). Each has its own subscription and maintenance burden.</li>
      <li><strong>Time to first useful site is misleading.</strong> "WordPress launches in a day" assumes a generic theme. A real service-business WordPress site that is fast, secure, and has decent local SEO usually takes 2&ndash;4 weeks of agency work and $3,000&ndash;$8,000.</li>
      <li><strong>Maintenance never ends.</strong> Even if your site is built well, plugin updates land weekly. Skip them and you are vulnerable; install them blindly and you risk breakage. Most small businesses pay an agency a $100&ndash;$300/month maintenance retainer just to keep WordPress running.</li>
    </ul>

